> {bi'ai} has already been proposed for {naku ka'e naku},
> which corresponds to {romu'ei} if {ka'e} is {su'omu'ei}.
> 
> I still don't understand the distinction you make between
> {ka'e} and {su'omu'ei}. Could you give an example in Lojban
> where one would be true and the other false?

I can give you a distinction between kakne and cumki:
cumki fa lenu mi zatfa'i la barda jamfu
kakne lenu mi zatfa'i la barda jamfu
The first is true, the latter false. I think anything which is
true for kakne is true for cumki, but not the other way around. I
would think cumki corresponds to one kind of su'omu'ei, and kakne
to another kind of su'omu'ei (similarly, nibli corresponds to one
kind of romu'ei, and nitcu or bilga correspond to another kind of
romu'ei).

As I said in the other thread, I'm not thoroughly convinced the
above is the best way to look at things, and my familiarity with
modal logic is minimal.

